In PHP, you can check if a MySQL query causes an error using the `mysqli_error()` function, which returns a string describing the last error that occurred on the defined connection. MySQL warnings do not generate PHP errors or exceptions, so they can't be caught in the same way.

But in this case, you can detect duplicate entries (which would be considered as an error, not a warning) in your PHP code before you perform your query. You can make a SELECT query first to see if the JobName already exists. If it does, then you can handle that in PHP without trying to perform an INSERT or UPDATE query, which would result in a MySQL error.

Here's a simplified example:

```php
$jobName = // the job name you want to add
$conn = new mysqli($servername, $username, $password, $dbname);

// check connection
if ($conn->connect_error) {
    die("Connection failed: " . $conn->connect_error);
}

$sql = "SELECT 1 FROM Jobs WHERE JobName = ? LIMIT 1";
$stmt = $conn->prepare($sql);
$stmt->bind_param("s", $jobName);
$stmt->execute();
$result = $stmt->get_result();

if ($result->num_rows > 0) {
    // JobName already exists, handle it here
} else {
    // Perform the insert operation as JobName doesn't exist
}
$stmt->close();
$conn->close();
```

In this code, if a row is returned in the SELECT query, it means the JobName already exists in the database. So you may decide to not perform INSERT/UPDATE operation and handle it accordingly.

